    {
      "id": "Genetics_chapter2_001",
      "title": "Properties of Distributions",
      "nav_label": "Properties of Distributions",
      "raw_title": "Genetics_chapter2_001 · Properties of Distributions",
      "level": 2,
      "depth": 1,
      "anchor": "genetics_chapter2_001",
      "text_preview": "Before delving into the genetics of quantitative variation, it is essential to have a basic understanding of statistics. The statistical concepts and techniques most frequently encountered in quantitative genetics are presented in this and the following chapter. For the reader with advanced training in statistical the…"
    },
    {
      "id": "Genetics_chapter2_002",
      "title": "PARAMETERS OF THE UNIVARIATE DISTRIBUTIONS",
      "nav_label": "PARAMETERS OF THE UNIVARIATE DISTRIBUTIONS",
      "raw_title": "Genetics_chapter2_002 · PARAMETERS OF THE UNIVARIATE DISTRIBUTIONS",
      "level": 2,
      "depth": 1,
      "anchor": "genetics_chapter2_002",
      "text_preview": "Characters that are studied by biologists are of three types. Traits that are distributed into a range of discrete classes, such as scale counts in fish or leaf number in plants, are called meristic characters. Those that are measured on a continuous scale are known as metric characters. Length, weight, and growth rat…"
    },
    {
      "id": "Genetics_chapter2_003",
      "title": "THE NORMAL DISTRIBUTION",
      "nav_label": "THE NORMAL DISTRIBUTION",
      "raw_title": "Genetics_chapter2_003 · THE NORMAL DISTRIBUTION",
      "level": 2,
      "depth": 1,
      "anchor": "genetics_chapter2_003",
      "text_preview": "When large data sets of the type compiled in Table 2.1 are displayed in the form of frequency histograms (Figure 2.1), they often approximate a bell-shaped distribution. Three famous mathematicians, DeMoivre (1738), LaPlace (1778), and Gauss (1809), worked out the properties of a very useful description of this form —…"
    },
    {
      "id": "Genetics_chapter2_004",
      "title": "THE NORMAL DISTRIBUTION / The Truncated Normal Distribution",
      "nav_label": "The Truncated Normal Distribution",
      "raw_title": "Genetics_chapter2_004 · THE NORMAL DISTRIBUTION / The Truncated Normal Distribution",
      "level": 2,
      "depth": 1,
      "anchor": "genetics_chapter2_004",
      "text_preview": "We now consider the properties of a subset of the normal distribution, specifically a tail of the distribution (Figure 2.2). Such a consideration is important in the extreme form of directional selection that is used by most plant and animal breeders. Under truncation selection, all individuals below a certain phenoty…"
    },
    {
      "id": "Genetics_chapter2_005",
      "title": "CONFIDENCE INTERVALS",
      "nav_label": "CONFIDENCE INTERVALS",
      "raw_title": "Genetics_chapter2_005 · CONFIDENCE INTERVALS",
      "level": 2,
      "depth": 1,
      "anchor": "genetics_chapter2_005",
      "text_preview": "Estimates such as $ \\bar{z} $ and $ \\operatorname{Var}(z) $ vary from one sample to the next because of sampling error, so it is useful to know how far an observed statistic is likely to deviate from the true parameter that is being estimated. Although the true values are unknown, if something is known about the sampl…"
    }
  ],
